I’ll inspect the measurement metadata and diffraction pattern first, then identify candidate phases and build a quantitative multiphase GSAS-II refinement. I’ll keep the submitted CIFs, profile, JSON, report, and the exact final project mutually consistent.

The chemistry points first toward calcium tungstates, but I’m treating residual oxides and alternate Ca–W–O stoichiometries as real candidates rather than assuming a single CaWO₄ product. I’m now extracting peak positions and checking the supplied structure library/index against that chemical space.

Peak accounting now strongly supports scheelite CaWO₄ plus unreacted low-temperature WO₃: the WO₃ triplet near 22.94/23.42/24.23° and its 26.44, 28.81, and 33.10/33.43° lines are distinct from the CaWO₄ series. I also see weak lines where CaO could occur, so I’ll test whether adding CaO is statistically and chemically justified rather than folding those counts into the background.

The line positions resolve an important ambiguity: the 28.54/28.81° pair belongs to two CaWO₄ reflections, not two phases. Applying the same ≈−0.16° instrument offset to the remaining lines matches monoclinic room-temperature WO₃ (P2₁/n), including the 33.10/33.43° split and the 41.31/41.76° pair. I’m moving from peak matching to full-profile trials now, including alternate WO₃ settings and an explicit CaO inclusion test.

The first two-phase refinement is converging and already drops the weighted profile residual from 35.5% to 18.4% once the cells and zero offset are allowed to adjust. The fitted scheelite cell stays essentially at the literature value; the WO₃ cell moves only modestly, which is a good sign that the phase assignment is not compensating through an unphysical lattice. I’m letting the profile/size stages finish before comparing models.

The initial full-profile result is 87.5 wt% CaWO₄ and 12.5 wt% WO₃, but I’m not accepting it yet: an unconstrained six-parameter profile fit produced nonphysical individual U/V/W terms even though the net peak widths remained valid. The model-comparison runs are in progress; after selecting the phase model I’ll use a restrained, lower-correlation profile parameterization so the reported fraction and uncertainties are defensible.

The alternatives are slower than the first pass because they are running side-by-side and some lower-symmetry models carry many more reflections. No decision is being made from elapsed time; I’m comparing the same objective profile statistics, refined cells, and whether any added phase receives a stable positive scale.

The CaO inclusion is chemically coherent, not just a small numerical improvement. The refined crystalline fractions are about 84.1% CaWO₄, 12.6% WO₃, and 3.2% CaO; the residual precursor mass ratio (WO₃:CaO ≈ 3.94) is essentially the stoichiometric 231.84:56.08 ratio (4.13) expected from unreacted 1:1 precursors. I’m retaining CaO provisionally and checking that its characteristic lines, cell, and scale remain stable under a physically constrained final profile model.

The preferred-orientation test is decisive: a mild [001] March–Dollase correction (coefficient ≈0.88) improves Rwp from 12.0% to 10.6%, while [100], [101], and [112] all fit worse. Refining atomic coordinates gives only a marginal gain and drives oxygen positions implausibly far, so I’m keeping literature coordinates fixed and treating the [001] texture correction as the justified intensity adjustment.

The final texture-corrected model is now numerically stable: repeated refinements converge without singularities (maximum shift/esd ≈0.02), and the minor CaO cell settles at ~4.819 Å, essentially the 4.817 Å reference value. The phase fractions are stable at roughly 84.3/12.4/3.3 wt%; profile-model alternatives shift them by about 1–2 wt%, which I’ll carry into the uncertainty discussion rather than reporting only the smaller covariance errors.

The final all-points refinement is complete and converged cleanly (maximum shift/esd 0.007). It gives 84.30 wt% CaWO₄, 12.40 wt% monoclinic WO₃, and 3.29 wt% CaO, with Rwp 10.59%. I’m doing a final cross-check now that the CIFs parse, the fractions sum to unity, `fit.csv` matches the arrays stored in `final.gpx`, and the JSON statistics reproduce from that project.

Identified three crystalline phases, normalized over crystalline material:

- CaWO₄ scheelite: 84.30 wt%
- Monoclinic WO₃: 12.40 wt%
- Cubic CaO: 3.29 wt%

Final fit: Rp 7.424%, Rwp 10.588%, GOF 4.721. Realistic model uncertainties are approximately ±2, ±1, and ±1 absolute wt%, respectively; CaO is least certain.

